## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React bails out of re-rendering only when props are **referentially equal** (`===`). Primitives compare by value, but objects, arrays, and functions compare by identity — a new reference is created on every render.

```jsx
// Every render creates a new object reference → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a new function reference → same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

The root cause: JavaScript creates a new object/function literal in memory each time the parent function body executes.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props haven't changed (shallow comparison).

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, style }) => <button style={style} onClick={onClick} />);
```

Use it when: a component re-renders often with the same props, and the render is non-trivial.

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a **function** across renders.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

Use it when: passing a callback to a `React.memo` component (otherwise memo is useless — the function reference changes every render anyway).

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a **computed value** across renders.

```jsx
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: theme.primary }), [theme.primary]);
```

Use it when: passing an object/array to a memoized child, or for an expensive computation.

**Rule of thumb:** `React.memo` without `useCallback`/`useMemo` on the props it receives is usually wasted effort.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context **value** changes reference — even if the data inside is identical.

```jsx
// BAD: new object every render → all consumers re-render on every parent render
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Fixes:
- **Split contexts** — separate stable values (dispatch/setters) from frequently-changing state.
- **Memoize the value object** with `useMemo`.

```jsx
// GOOD: value only changes when user actually changes
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
return <UserContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</UserContext.Provider>;
```

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...]);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items}
        onSelect={id => setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i.id !== id))}
        style={{ padding: 16 }}
      />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect, style }) { /* heavy render */ }
```

Every keystroke re-renders `Parent`, which creates a new `onSelect` function and a new `style` object, forcing `ExpensiveList` to re-render even though `items` didn't change.

**After** — stabilize references, wrap child in memo.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...]);

  const handleSelect = useCallback(
    id => setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i.id !== id)),
    [] // setItems is stable; no deps needed
  );

  const listStyle = useMemo(() => ({ padding: 16 }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} style={listStyle} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect, style }) {
  /* heavy render — now only runs when items actually changes */
});
```

`query` state changes no longer trigger `ExpensiveList` re-renders because `handleSelect` and `listStyle` are stable references, and `React.memo` sees no prop changes.

---

**When not to optimize:** profiling first. `useMemo`/`useCallback` have overhead too — a cheap component re-rendering occasionally is less costly than the complexity of memoization everywhere.
